    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-5649",
      "problem": "The Venn diagram below represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the probabilities of each event. Calculate $P(B|A).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273146/assets/d9d4a77a1978bbcbfff267fbebc749531198eb124f25c51dc96299b34aca6107.png)",
      "worked_solution": "To compute $P(B|A),$ we need to ask, \"what is the probability of $B$ occurring given that $A$ has occurred.\"\n\nSince we are assuming $A$ has occurred, we only consider the probabilities inside $A.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14273146/assets/4cf25c11c71635004393dcc73296391167fbb828009fe17decd62f2993ca5ddc.png)\n\nThe probability of event $A$ occurring is\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3+0.1=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, $P(B|A)$ can be interpreted as the proportion of $B$ that lies inside $A,$ relative to the size of $A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|A) = \\dfrac{0.1}{0.4} = 0.25.\n$$",
